content:
Flask uses routes to map URLs to Python functions.

from flask import Flask
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route('/')
def home():
    return 'Hello World'

@app.route('/about')
def about():
    return 'About page'

Dynamic routes:
@app.route('/user/<name>')
def user(name):
    return 'Hello ' + name

HTTP methods:
@app.route('/submit', methods=['GET','POST'])
def submit():
    if request.method == 'POST':
        pass

Run with: app.run(debug=True)

Flask uses Jinja2 to render HTML templates with dynamic data.

from flask import render_template

@app.route('/hello/<name>')
def hello(name):
    return render_template('hello.html', username=name)

In the HTML template:
<h1>Hello {{ username }}</h1>

Jinja2 syntax:
- Variables: {{ name }}
- Conditions: {% if condition %} ... {% endif %}
- Loops: {% for item in list %} ... {% endfor %}
- Filters: {{ name|upper }}

Templates are stored in the 'templates/' folder.

Flexbox is a one-dimensional layout system for arranging items.

Basic flex container:
.container {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 20px;
}

Key properties:
- justify-content: flex-start, center, space-between
- align-items: stretch, center, flex-end
- flex-direction: row, column
- flex-wrap: wrap, nowrap
- gap: space between items

On children:
- flex: 1 (grows to fill space)
- order: 1 (change order)

C++ is an object-oriented extension of C.

Basic class example:
class Student {
public:
    string name;
    int age;

    void display() {
        cout << name << ' ' << age;
    }
};

int main() {
    Student s1;
    s1.name = 'Alice';
    s1.age = 20;
    s1.display();
    return 0;
}

OOP concepts in C++:
- Encapsulation (private, public, protected)
- Inheritance (base and derived classes)
- Polymorphism (virtual functions)
- Abstraction (abstract classes)

JavaScript is the programming language of the web, used to make pages interactive.

Variables:
let name = 'Alice';
const PI = 3.14;
var age = 20;

Functions:
function greet(name) {
    return 'Hello, ' + name;
}

// Arrow function
const add = (a, b) => a + b;

DOM manipulation:
document.getElementById('myDiv').innerHTML = 'Hello';
document.querySelector('.btn').addEventListener('click', function() {
    alert('Clicked!');
});

JavaScript runs in browsers and also on servers (Node.js).

SQL (Structured Query Language) is used to work with relational databases.

Create a table:
CREATE TABLE students (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(50),
    age INT,
    grade CHAR(1)
);

Insert data:
INSERT INTO students VALUES (1, 'Alice', 20, 'A');

Select data:
SELECT * FROM students;
SELECT name, age FROM students WHERE grade = 'A';

Update data:
UPDATE students SET age = 21 WHERE id = 1;

Delete data:
DELETE FROM students WHERE id = 1;
